# Legacy Code Policy

Every project carries code written before these rules (imported shells, third-party packages, early prototypes). The rules describe **new and edited** code. They are not a mandate to rewrite what already ships.

## Rules

1. **Do the brief.** Do not refactor, rename, reformat, or "bring up to standard" code the brief does not touch — even when it plainly violates a rule. Untouched legacy is out of scope by default.
2. **Fix inside what you edit.** When the brief has you change a method, that method now counts as edited code and must satisfy the rules — fix the violation there. Do not extend the fix to sibling methods or the rest of the file; do not leave the violation in place because "it was already like that".
3. **Never suppress a guard to make legacy pass.** No new entries in a grandfather list, no `// *-guard: allow` markers, no `#pragma`, no widened exemption in the guard script, unless the brief asks for exactly that. If a guard fails on pre-existing code you did not change, report it under **Unverified** and stop — the user decides.
4. **Report what you saw.** Violations you noticed but did not fix go in the final summary as a short list (file, rule), so the team can schedule them. Noticing is free; fixing is a separate brief.
5. **Shell edits are logged.** When a project distinguishes imported shell code from game code (see its `AGENTS.md`), every shell edit gets its documented log line in the same change.

```csharp
// Brief: "make the payout label show two decimals"

// ❌ BAD — refactors the whole class, touches 8 unrelated methods
// ❌ BAD — leaves float money in the method you edited because it was already float

// ✅ GOOD — the edited method is now rule-compliant; the rest of the file is untouched
private void ShowPayout(decimal payout)
{
    _payoutText.text = payout.ToString("F2", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture);
}
```

## Agent checklist

- [ ] No edits outside the brief's scope; legacy left as found
- [ ] Every method actually edited satisfies the rules
- [ ] No new grandfather entries, allow markers, or guard exemptions without an explicit brief
- [ ] Violations noticed but not fixed listed in the summary
